Now I have all the information needed. Let me write the review findings.
The review has been written to `docs/reviews/types-physics/types-physics-08-tariff-demand-averaging-window-collapse.md`.

**Summary of findings:**

| Finding | Severity | Issue |
|---|---|---|
| #1 | **High** | Demand window silently collapses to 1 sample when `interval > demand_window`. Integer division `(15 * 60) / 3600 = 0` → capped to 1 → no averaging, no sub-step aggregation |
| #2 | **Medium** | No warning/error when step size exceeds demand window (the reverse case is checked) |
| #3 | **Low** | Periodic `running_sum` recomputation every 1000 pushes (unnecessary f64 drift guard) |
| #4-5 | None | Ratchet initialization and ring buffer off-by-one — both confirmed correct |

The key issue is at `billing.rs:125-130`: a 60-minute simulation step with a 15-minute demand window produces `capacity = 0`, silently clamped to `1`. An hourly average of 2.5 kW would be reported as demand instead of the true 10 kW 15-minute peak — a potential 4x undercharge with no user notification. The code comment at `evaluator.rs:184-187` defends this as "physically correct" but treats the step-average as if it were a peak, which is wrong for typical hourly building simulation data.
